What It Is
The Null-Model Protocol as a versioned software library. It implements the statistic, the scan families, the effective-null builder, and the full-pipeline simulator, with a test suite that reproduces the numbers in Section 13 of the Null-Model Protocol paper. It takes in a pipeline specification and data, and puts out the same outputs as the Full-Pipeline Simulator (App 9), as a library. It is for statisticians and developers.

Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Provide a stable library API for the Null-Model Protocol.

Implement the scan statistic Lambda(w) and the scan statistic Lambda_max.

Implement the scan families W_adj, W_pair, and fixed.

Implement the effective-null builder: apply a base null, a selection function, and a measurement kernel in the specified order, renormalise, and warn when the effective null has atoms.

Implement the full-pipeline simulator: reproduce the entire pipeline on synthetic data, return the empirical null distribution, the exceedance probability, the Monte Carlo uncertainty, and the empty-W fraction.

Implement the nuisance-estimation procedure, including the gap-exclusion loop and the truncated-likelihood treatment.

Implement the tail definition, whether >= or >.

Implement the multiplicity procedure.

Implement the support rules S1, S2, S3.

Implement the null models uniform, parametric, mixture, empirical, and KDE.

Implement the selection functions analytic, tabulated, and stepwise.

Implement the measurement kernels Gaussian (homoscedastic), Gaussian (heteroscedastic), uniform, exponential, and user-supplied.

Implement the dependence structures hierarchical simulation, block resampling, and fitted correlation model.

Implement clustering as a processing step.

Implement jittering.

Provide a test suite that reproduces the numbers in Section 13 of the Null-Model Protocol paper.

Support N up to 10^6 with direct Monte Carlo.

Provide the option for adaptive or importance sampling (deferred to a later version).

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic: same inputs produce same outputs.

Fast: run N simulations within a reasonable time (depends on N and the pipeline complexity).

Versioned: the library records the schema version and the library version.

Reproducible: the library records the seed and the random number generator.

Portable: works on Linux, macOS, and Windows.

Well-tested: the test suite covers all the worked examples.

Documented: API documentation and a specification reference.

Input Specification
The library has no runtime inputs of its own. It is a dependency that other apps import.

For simulation, the library takes:

Input 1. Pipeline specification. Required. A JSON object conforming to the schema produced by the Pipeline Specification Wizard (App 8).

Input 2. Data. Required. An array of numbers or a dataset object.

Input 3. Seed. Required. An integer.

Input 4. Options. Optional. An object with fields: N (integer), rng (string), progress (boolean), adaptive (boolean), output_dir (string).

Input 5. Scan family override. Optional. If provided, overrides the scan family in the specification.

Input 6. Null model override. Optional. If provided, overrides the null model in the specification.

For statistic computation, the library takes:

Input 7. Data. Required.

Input 8. Window. Required. A window with a lower bound and an upper bound.

Input 9. Effective null. Required.

Input 10. Specification. Required.

For effective-null building, the library takes:

Input 11. Base null. Required.

Input 12. Selection function. Optional.

Input 13. Measurement kernel. Optional.

Input 14. Order. Required if both selection and measurement are present.

Input 15. Support. Required.

Output Specification
For simulation:

Output 1. Result object. JSON. The observed statistic, the empirical null distribution, the exceedance probability, the Monte Carlo uncertainty, the empty-W fraction, the point mass at zero, the tail definition, the N, the seed, the runtime, the specification version, the library version, and any warnings.

For statistic computation:

Output 2. Statistic value. Number. The value of Lambda(w).

For effective-null building:

Output 3. Effective null object. The density function, the cumulative distribution function, the continuity status, the atoms, the discontinuities, and any warnings.

For the library:

Output 4. Function results. The return values of the public API functions.

Module-by-Module Specification
Module 1: Specification Loader
Reads and validates the pipeline specification against the schema produced by the Pipeline Specification Wizard (App 8). Provides access to the specification's fields.

Module 2: Data Loader
Reads and validates the data. Applies the transformation, the jittering, and the clustering specified. Assigns the support by the specified rule.

Module 3: Observed Statistic Calculator
Computes Lambda_obs on the observed data. Forms the scan family W with the specified g_min, boundary convention, and empty-W convention. Estimates nuisance parameters under the initial null. Selects the candidate window. Excludes it and re-estimates. Recomputes Lambda(w) for all w in W under the re-estimated effective null. Sets Lambda_obs to the maximum.

Module 4: Synthetic Data Generator
For each simulation i from 1 to N, generates synthetic data from the null in the space where the null, the selection function, and the measurement kernel are defined.

Applies dependence structure if specified.

Applies selection and measurement in the specified order.

Transforms if not already transformed.

Applies jittering with an independent draw from the same distribution if specified.

Applies clustering if applicable.

Assigns the support by the same rule, computed from the synthetic data under S2.

Module 5: Effective Null Builder
Applies the base null, the selection function, and the measurement kernel in the specified order. Renormalises. Returns the effective null. Warns when it has atoms.

Module 6: Scan Statistic Calculator
Forms the scan family W with the same g_min, boundary convention, and empty-W convention. Computes Lambda(w) for all w in W. Sets Lambda_max to the maximum.

Module 7: Nuisance Estimation
Estimates nuisance parameters under the initial null. Selects the candidate window. Excludes it and re-estimates with the truncated-likelihood treatment. Applies the gap-exclusion loop with the specified iteration count.

Module 8: Empirical Null Distribution Builder
Collects Lambda_max,1 through Lambda_max,N. Computes the empirical null distribution. Computes the point mass at zero.

Module 9: Exceedance Probability Calculator
Computes p = (1 + number of i with Lambda_max,i >= Lambda_obs) / (N + 1) if the tail definition is >=, or p = (1 + number of i with Lambda_max,i > Lambda_obs) / (N + 1) if the tail definition is >.

Module 10: Monte Carlo Uncertainty Calculator
Computes the standard error of p as approximately sqrt(p * (1 - p) / N).

Module 11: Empty-W Fraction Calculator
Computes the fraction of synthetic datasets with an empty W. Applies the empty-W convention.

Module 12: Multiplicity Handler
Applies the multiplicity procedure once, after the loop. Handles multiple nulls if specified.

Module 13: Result Formatter
Produces the result object.

Module 14: Reproducibility Recorder
Records the specification version, the library version, the seed, the RNG, the number of simulations, and the runtime.

